window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["notes"]=[{"id":"review-001","title":"al-Fātiḥa · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nṬībī coverage is only the completed first instalment, printed I:677–686. The remaining Fātiḥa commentary has not been translated in this release.\n\nREVIEW RECORD AND OPEN FLAGS\n1. Full first pass of the author’s sūrah commentary is now present. The opening of the entire book is a separate future unit.\n2. K001.12–13 restore the major K-DKA transcription omission. They are checked against the separate K-JK transcription and the actual embedded Arabic text in the Dubai scan, I:734–741. The modern editorial source note after the proverb in K-JK is excluded.\n3. The contrast مِلْك / مُلْك is ownership / kingship. This order is confirmed by the scanned vocalization and Turkish comparison. The preference for malik is Zamakhsharī’s, not an editorial choice to suppress the mālik reading.\n4. K001-U1: both transcriptions say “Raḥmān of this world and the next, and Raḥīm of this world.” The unexpected final “this world” is also supported by the Turkish text, p.76, and is retained; further Arabic scan collation is pending. Do not silently correct it.\n5. K001-U2: the vowel-assimilation examples and minor poetic/proper-name vocalizations require a second Arabic scan pass.\n6. Reports are translated as reports. No new independent judgment of authenticity is supplied. Ṭībī’s comments on the closing reports must be translated separately at the end of his Fātiḥa layer.\n7. Still needed: complete line-by-line scan comparison of K001.01–22 against ʿUbaykān and the YEK facing Arabic; a second English fidelity pass; the rest of Ṭībī I:677–770.\n8. Technical policy: iltifāt, badal, ḥāl, tawfīq, alṭāf, milk/mulk, and the actual Arabic reading variants are preserved where their distinctions carry the argument. Square-bracketed clarifications are editorial, not medieval source text.\n\n\n\n\nC04 TURKISH COMPARISON ADDENDUM\nThe complete Turkish Fātiḥa text, even-numbered pages 66–98, paragraphs 9–78, was read against this draft. The English poem was corrected to news concerning Abū al-Aswad, rather than transmission on his authority. Nibz and allaha were corrected in the lexical discussion. Arabic remains controlling; Turkish expansions, editorial hadith judgments, and occasional interpretive differences have not been silently incorporated. The Turkish numbered verse scheme excludes the opening basmala from its displayed 1–7 sequence; Arabic reference numbers in this draft retain the usual basmala-counting scheme.\n\nEND OF PART 1\n\nNEXT EXACT START\nPrinted I:687: قوله: (معنى اختصاص اسم الله بالابتداء)، اعلم أن التقديم إما لمجرد الاهتمام، أو مع الاختصاص...\nOpenITI page labels occur AFTER the text they label. Begin after PageV01P686, then distinguish the Kashshāf base block from Ṭībī’s commentary.\nThe next argument distinguishes simple emphasis from exclusive restriction, discusses pagan invocation and the treaty of al-Ḥudaybiya, and then addresses the evidentiary force of Q11:41.\n\nREVIEW NOTES\n- All ten scanned pages I:677–686 have been inspected. Source quotations have been kept attributed; they are not presented as independent statements by the present translator.\n- Al-Intiṣāf and al-Inṣāf are distinct titles. Do not merge them.\n- “The seventh verse” in the Ibn ʿAbbās report is retained. It counts the basmala as completing the number seven, not as relocating it to the sūrah’s end.\n- T001-U1 and T001-U2 remain open.\n- The modern Dubai footnotes sometimes criticize the hadith chains or supply manuscript variants. They are separate editorial material, not automatically part of Ṭībī’s own commentary.","source":"K I:1–19 · T I:677–686","scope":[{"record_id":"K001.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1–7","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.07","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.08","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.09","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:2–3","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.10","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:2–3","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.11","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:2–3","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.12","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:4","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.13","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:4","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.14","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:5","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.15","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:5","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.16","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:5","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.17","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:6","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.18","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:7","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.19","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:7","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.20","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:7","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.21","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1–7","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"},{"record_id":"K001.22","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1–7","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa"}]},{"id":"review-094","title":"al-Sharḥ · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nSOURCE AND REVIEW NOTES\nK094-U1: K-DKA's transcription has ʿumūm al-nubuwwa, “the full scope of prophethood,” where the Kashshāf quoted and discussed by Ṭībī has humūm al-nubuwwa, “the concerns of prophethood.” The latter is used here and the difference is retained for collation.\nK094-U2: K-DKA's transcription has a damaged or variant form in ʿUmar's saying; Ṭībī preserves and explicitly explains sabahlalan. Likewise, the threefold explanation of lifting the burden is rendered according to the clearer quoted form in Ṭībī.\nT094-U1: The text of the magnification argument on p. 499 is syntactically damaged around “required it to reach its utmost extent.” Visual inspection confirms the incomplete phrase أن يتناهى في in the printed edition itself. The translation renders the evident argumentative connection and does not claim to establish every missing word. This locus remains for comparison with another textual witness.\nT094-U2: Technical discussions of readings, grammatical permissibility, legal acknowledgment, and the authors' sectarian polemic are translated as their historical claims. The closing merit report and other transmitted reports have not been independently authenticated.","source":"K IV:770–772 · T XVI:492–503","scope":[{"record_id":"K094.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"94:1–4","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ"},{"record_id":"K094.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"94:1–4","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ"},{"record_id":"K094.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"94:4","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ"},{"record_id":"K094.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"94:5–6","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ"},{"record_id":"K094.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"94:5–6","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ"},{"record_id":"K094.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"94:7–8","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ"}]},{"id":"review-095","title":"al-Tīn · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL NOTES\nK095-U1: The derivative forms ammān and karrām are retained as morphological examples; the unvowelled electronic source alone does not establish their full vocalization. The chapter’s argument is about the city’s trustworthiness and security.\nT095-U1: XVI:505 prints عند الغذاء, “as food,” correcting the electronic الغداء, “the midday meal.” The source’s historical therapeutic claims are translated as quoted claims, not endorsed as medical guidance.\nK095-U2: The verse rendering gives an intelligible English question; K095.06 and T095.06 preserve the author’s more specific grammatical construal, “what makes you a liar on account of denying requital,” and its alternative address to the Prophet.","source":"K IV:773–774 · T XVI:504–508","scope":[{"record_id":"K095.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"95:1–8","segment_title":"al-Tīn"},{"record_id":"K095.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"95:1","segment_title":"al-Tīn"},{"record_id":"K095.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"95:2–3","segment_title":"al-Tīn"},{"record_id":"K095.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"95:4–5","segment_title":"al-Tīn"},{"record_id":"K095.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"95:6","segment_title":"al-Tīn"},{"record_id":"K095.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"95:7–8","segment_title":"al-Tīn"},{"record_id":"K095.07","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"95:1–8","segment_title":"al-Tīn"}]},{"id":"review-097","title":"al-Qadr · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL NOTES\nK097-U1: The first interpretative statement makes God the exclusive agent of revelation; Ṭībī’s explicit grammatical explanation confirms this. The Turkish comparator’s wording appears to make the Qurʾān itself the agent; it has not been followed.\nK097-U2: The DKA text supplies the two distinct explanations of the thousand months. The embedded base in the Ṭībī transcription telescopes part of their wording; the complete DKA passage is retained.\nT097-U1: Ṣila and mawṣūl in the discussion of the verbal noun concern the relation of a governing expression to its dependent material; the intervening hiya is the difficulty. The English renders the grammatical relation rather than implying an ordinary relative pronoun.","source":"K IV:780–780 · T XVI:522–525","scope":[{"record_id":"K097.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"97:1–5","segment_title":"al-Qadr"},{"record_id":"K097.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"97:1","segment_title":"al-Qadr"},{"record_id":"K097.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"97:1","segment_title":"al-Qadr"},{"record_id":"K097.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"97:1–3","segment_title":"al-Qadr"},{"record_id":"K097.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"97:4–5","segment_title":"al-Qadr"},{"record_id":"K097.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"97:1–5","segment_title":"al-Qadr"}]},{"id":"review-099","title":"al-Zalzalah · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL NOTES\nK099-U1: The electronic DKA text has بالنسيان, “by forgetfulness,” where the embedded Kashshāf text in Ṭībī has باللسان, “by the tongue.” The latter is required by the discussion of literal speech and is followed here; the Turkish comparator supports it.\nT099-U1: Printed XVI:536 confirms the nine-verse heading, as against DKA’s eight; no verse text is missing from this translation. The same page prints تطلع in the lexical gloss for jizʿāl. “Limps” is a provisional contextual correction to تظلع, explicitly distinguished from the printed reading. The word’s vocalization and gloss require a further lexical witness.\nT099-U2: Printed XVI:539 confirms that the grammar discussion changes its illustrative name from ʿAmr to Zayd between examples. The translation preserves that change; its grammatical argument is not silently rewritten.\nT099-U3: Printed XVI:545 reads بنصف القرآن, “as half the Qurʾān,” correcting the transcription’s بنف القرآن. This differs from the Kashshāf’s preceding four-recitation report; both authorial statements are retained.\nT099-U4: The claims about unbelievers, major sinners, expiation, and mitigation of punishment are the positions quoted and debated by the authors. The quotation from al-Intiṣāf and Ṭībī’s description of al-Zamakhsharī’s intention remain separately attributed.","source":"K IV:783–785 · T XVI:536–545","scope":[{"record_id":"K099.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:1–8","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"},{"record_id":"K099.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:1–2","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"},{"record_id":"K099.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:3–4","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"},{"record_id":"K099.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:1–4","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"},{"record_id":"K099.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:5","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"},{"record_id":"K099.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:6","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"},{"record_id":"K099.07","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:7–8","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"},{"record_id":"K099.08","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:1–8","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah"}]},{"id":"review-101","title":"al-Qāriʿah · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL NOTES\nT101-U1: The Ṭībī chapter heading says ten verses, while the DKA heading says eleven. This difference has not been silently regularized.\nK101-U1: The poetic imprecation is rendered literally in the base text, with its actual force of astonished praise explained in Ṭībī’s layer. The embedded base has yathūb, “returns,” where DKA has yaʾūb; the sense is the same.\nT101-U2: Printed XVI:556 confirms khuzyā, “disgraced,” against the electronic transcription’s defective خِزْيا form. The season explanation and the author’s preference for the first interpretation are retained without editorial reconciliation.","source":"K IV:789–790 · T XVI:554–557","scope":[{"record_id":"K101.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"101:1–11","segment_title":"al-Qāriʿah"},{"record_id":"K101.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"101:1–5","segment_title":"al-Qāriʿah"},{"record_id":"K101.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"101:6–8","segment_title":"al-Qāriʿah"},{"record_id":"K101.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"101:8–9","segment_title":"al-Qāriʿah"},{"record_id":"K101.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"101:10–11","segment_title":"al-Qāriʿah"},{"record_id":"K101.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"101:1–11","segment_title":"al-Qāriʿah"}]},{"id":"review-102","title":"al-Takāthur · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nSOURCE AND REVIEW NOTES\nK102-U1: The difficult poetic citation involving ḍimād, a shared attachment, is translated together with Ṭībī's complete lexical explanation and alternate reading. Printed p. 560 was inspected and confirms the wording of that explanation; the compressed verse still requires comparison with further witnesses. Bracketed wording makes the supplied sense visible. The Turkish version construes the participants differently; its interpretation has not replaced Ṭībī's explicit gloss khalīl = husband.\nK102-U2: K-DKA's transcription has a damaged phrase concerning conversion of wāw to hamza. The translation follows the grammatical explanation preserved in Ṭībī's quoted base and his citation from al-Zajjāj.\nT102-U1: The image “the stain of clay” and “the herald of union” belongs to the quoted mystical definition in al-ʿAwārif; it is retained rather than replaced by an explanatory summary.\nT102-U2: Ṭībī first notes evidence for a general questioning and then argues for the address's specific context. Both stages of his argument are preserved. The closing merit report and other reports await independent hadith review.","source":"K IV:791–792 · T XVI:558–564","scope":[{"record_id":"K102.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"102:1–8","segment_title":"al-Takāthur"},{"record_id":"K102.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"102:1–2","segment_title":"al-Takāthur"},{"record_id":"K102.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"102:3–5","segment_title":"al-Takāthur"},{"record_id":"K102.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"102:6–7","segment_title":"al-Takāthur"},{"record_id":"K102.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"102:8","segment_title":"al-Takāthur"}]},{"id":"review-103","title":"al-ʿAṣr · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL NOTE\nT103-U1: XVI:566 reads أرض واصية, “land whose vegetation is continuous”; the electronic text’s أوض is corrected from the scan. This is a transcription correction, not a translation of the modern editor’s apparatus.","source":"K IV:793–793 · T XVI:565–567","scope":[{"record_id":"K103.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"103:1–3","segment_title":"al-ʿAṣr"},{"record_id":"K103.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"103:1–3","segment_title":"al-ʿAṣr"},{"record_id":"K103.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"103:1–3","segment_title":"al-ʿAṣr"}]},{"id":"review-104","title":"al-Humazah · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL NOTES\nK104-U1: The third variant of “will be cast” is printed as an active verb with an object pronoun; the agent is not explained in the Arabic passage. The English preserves that indeterminacy rather than importing the Turkish comparator’s supplied subject, “wealth.”\nT104-U1: The discussion of wa-ʿadadahu moves between a verbal analysis and an accusative nominal analysis with an omitted verb. The cold-water example illustrates an understood “gave to drink”; the compact source wording and both analyses are retained.\nT104-U2: XVI:574 prints وأقر بدء إحراق كل أحد على قدر استحقاقه. “The commencement of each person’s burning is fixed according to what he deserves” is a provisional construal of this difficult phrase. The scan confirms the wording; further witnesses are needed.\nT104-U3: The main text at XVI:576 reads يلزم, “would follow,” in its final sentence; the printed apparatus records a witness with لا يلزم, “would not follow.” This reverses the implication. The translation follows the printed main text and retains this unresolved variant explicitly; it does not silently attribute a settled doctrine of everlasting punishment for every slanderer to Ṭībī.","source":"K IV:794–796 · T XVI:568–576","scope":[{"record_id":"K104.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"104:1–9","segment_title":"al-Humazah"},{"record_id":"K104.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"104:1","segment_title":"al-Humazah"},{"record_id":"K104.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"104:2","segment_title":"al-Humazah"},{"record_id":"K104.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"104:3","segment_title":"al-Humazah"},{"record_id":"K104.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"104:4–7","segment_title":"al-Humazah"},{"record_id":"K104.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"104:8–9","segment_title":"al-Humazah"},{"record_id":"K104.07","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"104:1–9","segment_title":"al-Humazah"}]},{"id":"review-105","title":"al-Fīl · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL AND REVIEW NOTES\nK105-U1: The conflicting elephant counts and the clause “and it was alone” occur in the printed base at XVI:578 as well as the transcription. The English marks the alternative rather than inventing a single consistent count.\nT105-U1: The electronic wording says “with dotted ghayn” when explaining عرض, which begins with ʿayn. The intelligible word is rendered ʿurḍ; the letter-name needs scan/manuscript collation before finalisation.\nK105-U2: The DKA poem reads عدوا where Ṭībī’s gloss explicitly discusses غدوا, “soon/tomorrow”; the latter is followed and the difference recorded.","source":"K IV:797–799 · T XVI:577–584","scope":[{"record_id":"K105.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"105:1–5","segment_title":"al-Fīl"},{"record_id":"K105.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"105:1–2","segment_title":"al-Fīl"},{"record_id":"K105.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"105:1–2","segment_title":"al-Fīl"},{"record_id":"K105.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"105:1–3","segment_title":"al-Fīl"},{"record_id":"K105.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"105:3–5","segment_title":"al-Fīl"}]},{"id":"review-106","title":"Quraysh · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL AND REVIEW NOTES\nK106-U1 / T106-U1: The camel verse has zahw in the printed Dubai base and rahw in the DKA transcription. Both are preserved and explained. The difficult printed phrase الزهو غير الإدراك appears in the transcription as well; it is not securely established as a transcription error. The bracketed reconstruction of the cited verse and its relation to the adjacent lexical explanations require further fine collation before final publication. The provisional account above retains all the lexical alternatives rather than suppressing the difficulty.\nT106-U2: The reading “al-Zubayr, from al-Zajjāj” occurs in the print; the modern apparatus notes a witness omission. It remains attributed as printed, without asserting a resolved identification.","source":"K IV:800–802 · T XVI:585–590","scope":[{"record_id":"K106.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"106:1–4","segment_title":"Quraysh"},{"record_id":"K106.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"106:1–2","segment_title":"Quraysh"},{"record_id":"K106.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"106:1–2","segment_title":"Quraysh"},{"record_id":"K106.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"106:2–4","segment_title":"Quraysh"}]},{"id":"review-107","title":"al-Māʿūn · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nREVIEW FLAGS\nT107-U1: The six-verse/Medinan heading differs from the base’s seven-verse alternatives. It is preserved as transmitted, not adopted as a settled verse count.\nT107-U2: The elaborate argument on ʿan/fī and legal responsibility is fully retained. Printed XVI:597 was inspected; further comparison of the quoted authorities is pending.","source":"K IV:803–805 · T XVI:591–599","scope":[{"record_id":"K107.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"107:1–7","segment_title":"al-Māʿūn"},{"record_id":"K107.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"107:1–7","segment_title":"al-Māʿūn"},{"record_id":"K107.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"107:1–5","segment_title":"al-Māʿūn"},{"record_id":"K107.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"107:6–7","segment_title":"al-Māʿūn"}]},{"id":"review-108","title":"al-Kawthar · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL AND REVIEW NOTES\nK108-U1: The first electronic Kashshāf witness prints أعطيناك despite immediately saying “with nūn.” The embedded Kashshāf in Ṭībī has أنطيناك, which supplies the intended dialectal form.\nT108-U1: The electronic text drops the oath and the Prophet’s reply in the al-Rubayyiʿ report. The full words above are restored from printed XVI:603, visually inspected.\nT108-U2: The printed phrase وأن المعطى لم يكن عظيما، إلا أن المعطي عظيم remains syntactically difficult. The provisional concessive rendering is retained with an explicit flag; do not silently rewrite it as an unqualified claim about the gift’s size. The following sentence expressly describes both gift and Giver as great.\nT108-U3: The phrase ليدمج معنى معطى قطع النفس in the scan/transcription is also awkward. “So as to incorporate the meaning of cutting the soul off” renders the evident relation of sacrifice to self-denial, but the extra معطى needs further textual collation.\nK108-U2: Jamʿ is Muzdalifa in the Arabic. The Turkish comparison’s phrasing places the dawn prayer at Minā; the English follows the Arabic distinction between Jamʿ and Minā.","source":"K IV:806–807 · T XVI:600–605","scope":[{"record_id":"K108.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"108:1–3","segment_title":"al-Kawthar"},{"record_id":"K108.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"108:1","segment_title":"al-Kawthar"},{"record_id":"K108.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"108:2","segment_title":"al-Kawthar"},{"record_id":"K108.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"108:3","segment_title":"al-Kawthar"},{"record_id":"K108.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"108:1–3","segment_title":"al-Kawthar"}]},{"id":"review-109","title":"al-Kāfirūn · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nSOURCE AND REVIEW NOTES\nT109-U1: The working transcription drops “before” in the sentence on p. 608 asserting worship according to a revealed law before the mission, and contains minor damaged forms in the surrounding quotations. The scan was inspected: قبل المبعث is present in print and confirms the restoration. The complete closing pages 611–612 were also visually checked.\nT109-U2: Ṭībī both preserves and criticizes al-Zamakhsharī's assertion about worship before the mission. The assertion has not been silently rewritten to agree with the criticism. Similarly, both positions on abrogation and all six explanations of the apparent repetition are retained.\nT109-U3: Punctuation distinguishing al-Rāzī's quoted argument from Ṭībī's interventions is editorial in this translation. The interventions explicitly introduced as “I say” remain attributed to Ṭībī.","source":"K IV:808–809 · T XVI:606–612","scope":[{"record_id":"K109.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"109:1–6","segment_title":"al-Kāfirūn"},{"record_id":"K109.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"109:1","segment_title":"al-Kāfirūn"},{"record_id":"K109.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"109:2–5","segment_title":"al-Kāfirūn"},{"record_id":"K109.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"109:6","segment_title":"al-Kāfirūn"}]},{"id":"review-110","title":"al-Naṣr · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nTEXTUAL AND REVIEW NOTES\nT110-U1: The electronic text omits the quotation at 62:2; “and teaches them the Book and wisdom” is restored from printed XVI:615 (physical PDF page 614).\nK110-U1: The two-year survival report conflicts with the Farewell-Pilgrimage dating; Ṭībī’s chronological criticism is preserved separately. No historical reconciliation is imposed.\nK110-U2: Nafīr in the DKA electronic text is rendered following nafas in Ṭībī’s printed base and lexical explanation; the variant is retained here for further collation.\nT110-U2: The supplied volume XVI scan skips printed p. 613: physical page 612 shows printed 612, and physical page 613 shows printed 614. The chapter opening on printed p. 613 is present in the electronic transcription and is translated here; a replacement scan is still needed for its visual collation. Do not apply a single page offset throughout this PDF.","source":"K IV:810–812 · T XVI:613–621","scope":[{"record_id":"K110.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"110:1–3","segment_title":"al-Naṣr"},{"record_id":"K110.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"110:1","segment_title":"al-Naṣr"},{"record_id":"K110.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"110:2","segment_title":"al-Naṣr"},{"record_id":"K110.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"110:3","segment_title":"al-Naṣr"},{"record_id":"K110.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"110:1–3","segment_title":"al-Naṣr"},{"record_id":"K110.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"110:1–3","segment_title":"al-Naṣr"}]},{"id":"review-111","title":"al-Masad · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nREVIEW FLAGS\nT111-U1: The transmitted camel verse has rāhiq; the modern apparatus records zāhiq. The author’s own gloss explains rāhiq, retained here. His attribution of the completion to al-Zajjāj is preserved, though the editor disputes it.\nT111-U2: The printed transition الأصهب before the al-Muṭliʿ quotation is awkward; no missing prose has been invented. The explanation of aṣhab is fully translated where it occurs.","source":"K IV:813–816 · T XVI:622–631","scope":[{"record_id":"K111.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"111:1–5","segment_title":"al-Masad"},{"record_id":"K111.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"111:1","segment_title":"al-Masad"},{"record_id":"K111.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"111:2","segment_title":"al-Masad"},{"record_id":"K111.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"111:3–4","segment_title":"al-Masad"},{"record_id":"K111.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"111:5","segment_title":"al-Masad"}]},{"id":"review-112","title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nEND OF TRANSLATED AUTHORIAL TEXT\nReview: full draft; one complete scan pass over XVI 632–643; one Turkish base-text comparison over VI 1460–1467; no independent external review. Four substantive interpretive/source flags remain (T112-U1–U4). Subsequent passes must retain both authors' arguments and all quoted authorities, even where they conflict.","source":"K IV:817–819 · T XVI:632–643","scope":[{"record_id":"K112.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"112:1–4","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ"},{"record_id":"K112.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"112:1","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ"},{"record_id":"K112.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"112:1–2","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ"},{"record_id":"K112.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"112:1–4","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ"},{"record_id":"K112.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"112:1–4","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ"},{"record_id":"K112.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"112:4","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ"},{"record_id":"K112.07","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"112:1–4","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ"}]},{"id":"review-113","title":"al-Falaq · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nEND OF TRANSLATED AUTHORIAL TEXT\nFull first pass; one complete Arabic scan pass and one Turkish base-text comparison. Three unresolved readings remain. The dispute about sorcery is preserved as a dispute between the authors and their quoted authorities; it has not been harmonized.","source":"K IV:820–822 · T XVI:644–651","scope":[{"record_id":"K113.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"113:1–5","segment_title":"al-Falaq"},{"record_id":"K113.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"113:1","segment_title":"al-Falaq"},{"record_id":"K113.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"113:2","segment_title":"al-Falaq"},{"record_id":"K113.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"113:3","segment_title":"al-Falaq"},{"record_id":"K113.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"113:4","segment_title":"al-Falaq"},{"record_id":"K113.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"113:4–5","segment_title":"al-Falaq"},{"record_id":"K113.07","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"113:1–5","segment_title":"al-Falaq"}]},{"id":"review-114","title":"al-Nās · source and review notes · inherited record","content":"INHERITED C06 RECORD. Earlier scan claims have not been independently reconfirmed in this pass. Current corrections supersede provisional renderings below; consult the C07 review register.\n\nEND OF TRANSLATED AUTHORIAL TEXT\nFull first pass; one complete Arabic scan pass and one Turkish base-text comparison. Three principal interpretive/source flags remain, plus the “two classes/attributes” reading. The separate book epilogue is pending and is not part of this completion claim.","source":"K IV:823–825 · T XVI:652–656","scope":[{"record_id":"K114.01","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"114:1–6","segment_title":"al-Nās"},{"record_id":"K114.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"114:1","segment_title":"al-Nās"},{"record_id":"K114.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"114:1–3","segment_title":"al-Nās"},{"record_id":"K114.04","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"114:4–5","segment_title":"al-Nās"},{"record_id":"K114.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"114:6","segment_title":"al-Nās"},{"record_id":"K114.06","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"114:5–6","segment_title":"al-Nās"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-1","title":"K001.03 · C07 correction","content":"نحسد الإنس الطعاما: envy is the direct lexical sense and is confirmed by Ṭībī’s explanation in T001.12; the existing begrudge is less exact.\n\nBefore: We begrudge mankind their food.\n\nAfter: We envy human beings their food.","source":"C07 review register · K001.03","scope":[{"record_id":"K001.03","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa · The omitted governing verb"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-2","title":"K001.12 · C07 correction","content":"Existing rendering reverses the two nouns. Al-Jurjānī’s primary ḥāshiya explicitly reads الملك بالضم يعم والملك بالكسر يخص; Ṭībī’s corresponding lemma is also vocalized المُلك يعم، والمِلك يخص.\n\nBefore: ownership (milk, مِلْك) is general, whereas kingship (mulk, مُلْك) is particular\n\nAfter: kingship (mulk, مُلْك) is general, whereas ownership (milk, مِلْك) is particular","source":"C07 review register · K001.12","scope":[{"record_id":"K001.12","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"1:4","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa · Mālik, malik, and the Day of Recompense"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-3","title":"T001.05 · C07 correction","content":"The source says قال في الشعلة, introducing a cited work rather than naming an author as the speaker.\n\nBefore: Al-Shuʿla says:\n\nAfter: In al-Shuʿla (الشعلة) it is stated:","source":"C07 review register · T001.05","scope":[{"record_id":"K001.02","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa · The basmala as a verse"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-4","title":"T001.08 · C07 correction","content":"No printed-page image was inspected in this audit; the electronic reading الذابح is confirmed, but a print-check claim is not.\n\nBefore: the print and transcription actually read al-dhābiḥ\n\nAfter: the electronic transcription reads al-dhābiḥ","source":"C07 review register · T001.08","scope":[{"record_id":"K001.03","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa · The omitted governing verb"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-5","title":"T001.09 · C07 correction","content":"The present evidence is an electronic transcription; no scan is available to substantiate printed.\n\nBefore: is retained as printed\n\nAfter: is retained as transcribed","source":"C07 review register · T001.09","scope":[{"record_id":"K001.03","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"1:1","segment_title":"al-Fātiḥa · The omitted governing verb"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-6","title":"T094.01 · C07 correction","content":"revised_translation\n\nBefore: On “it affirms and establishes the expansion”: He repudiates the absence of expansion. Once that absence is repudiated, expansion is established, because the interrogative hamza expresses repudiation; repudiation is negation, and negation applied to negation becomes affirmation. The hamza may not be treated here as a question seeking acknowledgment (taqrīr).\n\nAfter: Meccan; eight verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n\nOn “it affirms and establishes the expansion”: He repudiates the absence of expansion. Once that absence is repudiated, expansion is established, because the interrogative hamza expresses repudiation; repudiation is negation, and negation applied to negation becomes affirmation. The hamza may not be treated here as a question seeking acknowledgment (taqrīr).","source":"C07 review register · T094.01","scope":[{"record_id":"K094.02","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"94:1–4","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ · Expansion and the burden"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-7","title":"T094.04 · C07 correction","content":"revised_translation\n\nBefore: On “he read a-lam nashraḥa, with fatḥa on the ḥāʾ”: its original form is nashraḥan. The nūn was omitted and the fatḥa on the ḥāʾ retained as an indication of it. This is in al-Muntaqā.\nIbn Jinnī said: “A-lam nashraḥa, with fatḥa on the ḥāʾ, is transmitted from Abū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr. Ibn Mujāhid said: ‘This is wholly impermissible.’” Ibn Jinnī said: “The obvious position, and what accords with customary usage, is as Ibn Mujāhid states. Something like it nevertheless occurs in what I read to Abū ʿAlī in Abū Zayd's Nawādir:\n‘From which of death's two days shall I flee—\na day not decreed, or a day decreed?’\nIt is said that he intended lam yuqdar-an, with the light emphatic nūn. In our view omitting it is impermissible, because the emphatic nūn is most closely allied to expansion and elaboration, not concision and abbreviation. In Abū Zayd's Nawādir there is another verse, said to be fabricated:\n‘Strike away the anxieties that visit you by night,\nas with a sword you strike a horse's forelock.’\nHe intended iḍrib-an, with the light nūn, and omitted it.”\n\nAfter: On “he read a-lam nashraḥa, with fatḥa on the ḥāʾ”: its original form is nashraḥan. The nūn was omitted and the fatḥa on the ḥāʾ retained as an indication of it. This is in al-Muntaqā.\nIbn Jinnī said: “A-lam nashraḥa, with fatḥa on the ḥāʾ, is transmitted from Abū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr. Ibn Mujāhid said: ‘This is wholly impermissible.’” Ibn Jinnī said: “The obvious position, and what accords with customary usage, is as Ibn Mujāhid states. Something like it nevertheless occurs in what I read to Abū ʿAlī in Abū Zayd's Nawādir:\n‘From which of death's two days shall I flee—\na day not decreed, or a day decreed?’\nIt is said that he intended lam yuqdar-an, with the light emphatic nūn. In our view omitting it is impermissible, because the emphatic nūn is most closely allied to expansion and elaboration, not concision and abbreviation. In Abū Zayd's Nawādir there is another verse, said to be fabricated:\n‘Strike away the anxieties that visit you by night,\nas with a sword you strike the crown of a horse's head.’\nHe intended iḍrib-an, with the light nūn, and omitted it.”","source":"C07 review register · T094.04","scope":[{"record_id":"K094.02","segment_index":4,"quran_context":"94:1–4","segment_title":"al-Sharḥ · Expansion and the burden"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-8","title":"T095.02 · C07 correction","content":"revised_translation\n\nBefore: On “removes decay”: one says ḥafirat asnānuhu ḥafaran when the sockets of his teeth—that is, their roots—have deteriorated. One also says ḥafarat ḥafran; ḥafra denotes a single occurrence.\n\nOn “and so is amīn; ammān is also said”: that is, they say, “in a trustworthy place.”\n\nAfter: On “removes decay”: one says ḥafirat asnānuhu ḥafaran when the sockets of his teeth—that is, their roots—have deteriorated. One also says ḥafarat ḥafran; ḥafra denotes a single occurrence.\n\nOn “and so is amīn; ammān is also said”: that is, they use ammān (أمان) in place of amīn (أمين).","source":"C07 review register · T095.02","scope":[{"record_id":"K095.03","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"95:2–3","segment_title":"al-Tīn · Sīnīn, the secure city, and the blessed places"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-9","title":"T097.05 · C07 correction","content":"revised_translation\n\nBefore: On “it is nothing but safety”: he means that hiya, “it,” is the subject and salām, “peace,” the predicate. The predicate is placed first, and the night is made peace itself, to convey exclusivity.\n\nThe author of al-Kashf says: “Hiya is a subject and salām a preceding predicate. Salām has the sense of an active participle: it brings peace. This interpretation is necessary if ḥattā, ‘until,’ is to depend on it. If salām were taken as a verbal noun, ḥattā could not depend on it, because one may not separate a governing expression from its complement. It may instead depend on ‘the angels descend.’ Hiya cannot be the subject with the phrase beginning ḥattā as its predicate, for that would convey no useful information: every night has this characteristic [of lasting until dawn].”\n\nAfter: On “it is nothing but safety”: he means that hiya, “it,” is the subject and salām, “peace,” the predicate. The predicate is placed first, and the night is made peace itself, to convey exclusivity.\n\nThe author of al-Kashf says: “Hiya is a subject and salām a preceding predicate. Salām has the sense of an active participle: it brings peace. This interpretation is necessary if ḥattā, ‘until,’ is to depend on it. If salām were taken as a verbal noun, ḥattā could not depend on it, because the verbal noun may not be separated from its dependent complement [here by hiya]. It may instead depend on ‘the angels descend.’ Hiya cannot be the subject with the phrase beginning ḥattā as its predicate, for that would convey no useful information: every night has this characteristic [of lasting until dawn].”","source":"C07 review register · T097.05","scope":[{"record_id":"K097.05","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"97:4–5","segment_title":"al-Qadr · The descent and the peace of the night"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-10","title":"K099.02 · C07 correction","content":"revised_translation\n\nBefore: Zilzālahā is read with kasra or fatḥa on the zāy. With kasra it is a verbal noun; with fatḥa, a noun. Among word-patterns, faʿlāl with fatḥa occurs only in a reduplicated form.\n\nIf you ask what “its shaking” means in the possessive construction, I answer: the shaking that befits it in accordance with wisdom and God’s will—the violent shaking beyond which there is none. Compare: “Honour the pious man with his honour, and abase the impious man with his abasement,” meaning the honour and abasement they respectively deserve. Or it means its entire shaking, every degree of shaking possible for it.\n\nAthqāl is the plural of thiql, household goods, as in “They carry your burdens” [16:7]. He makes the things buried within the earth its burdens.\n\nAfter: Zilzālahā is read with kasra or fatḥa on the zāy. With kasra it is a verbal noun; with fatḥa, a noun. Among word-patterns, faʿlāl with fatḥa occurs only in a reduplicated form.\n\nIf you ask what “its shaking” means in the possessive construction, I answer: the shaking that befits it in accordance with wisdom and God’s will—the violent shaking beyond which there is none. Compare: “Honour the pious man with his honour, and abase the impious man with his abasement,” meaning the honour and abasement they respectively deserve. Or it means its entire shaking, every degree of shaking possible for it.\n\nAthqāl is the plural of thaqal (ثَقَل), household goods, as in “They carry your burdens” [16:7]. He makes the things buried within the earth its burdens.","source":"C07 review register · K099.02","scope":[{"record_id":"K099.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"99:1–2","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah · The shaking and the burdens"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-11","title":"T099.01 · C07 correction","content":"revised_translation\n\nBefore: Medinan; nine verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful. [The embedded Kashshāf heading instead records disagreement about the place of revelation; both headings here give nine verses.]\n\nOn “among word-patterns, faʿlāl with fatḥa occurs only in a reduplicated form,” al-Kawāshī has: “Nāqa jizʿāl occurs, meaning a she-camel which [limps; the printed text here reads ‘looks out/climbs’], as does qasṭāl, a word for dust; neither is reduplicated. It is said that Bahrām and Shahrām are foreign.” As for qahqār, it is a weak linguistic form. Al-Ṣiḥāḥ has: “Qahqarr, with doubled rāʾ, is hard stone. Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā alone used to say qahqār.”\n\nAfter: Medinan; nine verses. In the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful. [The embedded Kashshāf heading instead records disagreement about the place of revelation; both headings here give nine verses.]\n\nOn “among word-patterns, faʿlāl with fatḥa occurs only in a reduplicated form,” al-Kawāshī has: “The expression nāqa جزعال occurs, meaning a she-camel which [probably ‘limps’; the electronic text reads تطلع, ‘looks out/climbs’], as does qasṭāl, a word for dust; neither is reduplicated. It is said that Bahrām and Shahrām are foreign.” As for qahqār, it is a weak linguistic form. Al-Ṣiḥāḥ has: “Qahqarr, with doubled rāʾ, is hard stone. Aḥmad ibn Yaḥyā alone used to say qahqār.”","source":"C07 review register · T099.01","scope":[{"record_id":"K099.02","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"99:1–2","segment_title":"al-Zalzalah · The shaking and the burdens"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-12","title":"T105.05 · C07 correction","content":"يحصل describes the arising of entitlement to praise; the current discerned supplies an epistemic verb absent from the Arabic. The abstract phrasing avoids supplying an unstated subject of entitlement, and the contrast between كيفيات and ذوات is preserved.\n\nBefore: Desert of praise is discerned by seeing modes, not merely entities.\n\nAfter: Entitlement to praise arises only from observing the modes, not from observing the entities.","source":"C07 review register · T105.05","scope":[{"record_id":"K105.04","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"105:1–3","segment_title":"al-Fīl · The birds and “Have you not seen?”"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-13","title":"T106.03 · C07 correction","content":"Explicitly label the inherited conjecture; the retrieved electronic witness does not establish it.\n\nBefore: [The cited wording is] al-zahw ghayra l-awārik.\n\nAfter: [Earlier provisional reconstruction of the cited wording: al-zahw ghayra l-awārik. The retrieved electronic witness instead reads الزهو غير الإدراك; the wording remains unresolved.]","source":"C07 review register · T106.03","scope":[{"record_id":"K106.02","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"106:1–2","segment_title":"Quraysh · The journeys and variant forms"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-14","title":"K111.02 · C07 correction","content":"Corrected after electronic comparison.\n\nBefore: Fulayta ibn Qāsim\n\nAfter: Falīta ibn Qāsim (فَلِيتة بن قاسم)","source":"C07 review register · K111.02","scope":[{"record_id":"K111.02","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"111:1","segment_title":"al-Masad · The warning on al-Ṣafā and his byname"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-15","title":"K111.05 · C07 correction","content":"Corrected after electronic comparison.\n\nBefore: carrying and tying up the thorns like wood-gatherers\n\nAfter: carrying that bundle of thorns and fastening it about her neck, as wood-gatherers do","source":"C07 review register · K111.05","scope":[{"record_id":"K111.05","segment_index":0,"quran_context":"111:5","segment_title":"al-Masad · The rope, the taunt, and punishment"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-16","title":"T111.01 · C07 correction","content":"Corrected after electronic comparison.\n\nBefore: tabban lahu and tabba lahu\n\nAfter: tabban lahu (تَبًّا له) and tabbun lahu (تَبٌّ له)","source":"C07 review register · T111.01","scope":[{"record_id":"K111.01","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"111:1–5","segment_title":"al-Masad · Heading, verses, and destruction"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-17","title":"T111.01 · C07 correction","content":"Corrected after electronic comparison.\n\nBefore: ʿajizat and ʿajjazat\n\nAfter: ʿajazat (عَجَزَتْ) and ʿajjazat (عَجَّزَتْ)","source":"C07 review register · T111.01","scope":[{"record_id":"K111.01","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"111:1–5","segment_title":"al-Masad · Heading, verses, and destruction"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-18","title":"T111.04 · C07 correction","content":"Corrected after electronic comparison.\n\nBefore: Fulayta has fatḥa on fāʾ and kasra on lām\n\nAfter: Falīta (فَلِيتة) has fatḥa on fāʾ and kasra on lām","source":"C07 review register · T111.04","scope":[{"record_id":"K111.02","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"111:1","segment_title":"al-Masad · The warning on al-Ṣafā and his byname"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-19","title":"T112.12 · C07 correction","content":"Al-Baghdadi explicitly glosses the poetic phrase as الإقامة إلى وقت الصباح; the earlier literal 'for a morning' is less precise.\n\nBefore: I resolved to stay for a morning.\n\nAfter: I resolved to stay until morning.","source":"C07 review register · T112.12","scope":[{"record_id":"K112.07","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"112:1–4","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · If you ask, “Why is this sūrah the equivalent of the whole Qurʾān, despite its shortness and the clo"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-20","title":"T112.12 · C07 correction","content":"Replace the now-outdated claim that the half-line lacks a checked context.\n\nBefore: [REVIEW T112-U3: The isolated half-line إقامة ذي صباح lacks its full poetic context. The literal provisional rendering above should be checked against the poem.]\n\nAfter: [SOURCE CHECK T112-U3: Al-Baghdādī, Khizānat al-adab III:86–88, records the full line and explains إقامة ذي صباح as remaining until morning. The rendering follows that explanation; it does not establish every detail of the poem's attribution or context.]","source":"C07 review register · T112.12","scope":[{"record_id":"K112.07","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"112:1–4","segment_title":"al-Ikhlāṣ · If you ask, “Why is this sūrah the equivalent of the whole Qurʾān, despite its shortness and the clo"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-21","title":"T113.01 · C07 correction","content":"The printed/transcribed Tibi quote is corrupt here; Baydawi's quoted source explicitly has محاكاة فاتحة يوم القيامة. Brackets identify source-based restoration rather than a verified Tibi reading.\n\nBefore: evokes good through the Day of Resurrection\n\nAfter: [resembles the opening of the Day of Resurrection]","source":"C07 review register · T113.01","scope":[{"record_id":"K113.02","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"113:1","segment_title":"al-Falaq · Al-falaq and al-faraq mean morning, because night is split and parted away from it: faʿal has the me"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-22","title":"T113.01 · C07 correction","content":"Record new primary quotation-source evidence and retain witness distinction.\n\nBefore: [REVIEW T113-U1: The scanned quotation at p.645 has difficult wording محاكاة الخير بيوم القيامة and a final word read provisionally as تربية. OpenITI gives قريبة. The contextual rendering of this final clause is provisional and must be checked against al-Qāḍī's source.]\n\nAfter: [SOURCE CHECK T113-U1: Ṭībī's quoted text has the difficult محاكاة الخير بيوم القيامة and the electronic text ends with قريبة. Al-Bayḍāwī, Anwār al-tanzīl V:348, reads محاكاة فاتحة يوم القيامة and تربية. The bracketed resurrection clause follows that quoted source; تربية also corroborates the earlier provisional reading. These are source-based restorations, not a newly collated Ṭībī manuscript reading.]","source":"C07 review register · T113.01","scope":[{"record_id":"K113.02","segment_index":1,"quran_context":"113:1","segment_title":"al-Falaq · Al-falaq and al-faraq mean morning, because night is split and parted away from it: faʿal has the me"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-23","title":"T113.07 · C07 correction","content":"Al-Raghib's quoted source has ويمرض ويصح من حيث هو بشر لا من حيث هو نبي and لو وجد للسحر تأثير. The existing provisional reading gives صح a different syntactic role. Brackets show restoration from the quotation source.\n\nBefore: while remaining sound in his prophetic capacity. It would impair prophethood only [if]\n\nAfter: [and recovered, inasmuch as he was human, not inasmuch as he was a prophet]. It would impair prophethood only [if]","source":"C07 review register · T113.07","scope":[{"record_id":"K113.05","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"113:4","segment_title":"al-Falaq · Al-naffāthāt are women, souls, or groups of female sorcerers who tie knots in threads, blow upon the"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-24","title":"T113.07 · C07 correction","content":"The quoted source resolves the intended contrast and conditional without erasing the Tibi witness problem.\n\nBefore: [REVIEW T113-U2: The scan p.648 reads فيصح من حيث نبي and then أو وجد للسحر تأثير. The first is provisionally read as preservation of prophetic soundness; “[if]” expresses the apparently intended conditional of the second, whose printed أو is problematic. Check al-Rāghib's source before removing the flag. At p.649 the scan has بما يقع, correcting OpenITI's بما لا يقع, which reverses the argument.]\n\nAfter: [SOURCE CHECK T113-U2: The earlier scan report gives فيصح من حيث نبي and أو وجد للسحر تأثير. Al-Rāghib's own tafsīr of 2:102 reads ويمرض ويصح من حيث هو بشر لا من حيث هو نبي and لو وجد للسحر تأثير. The bracketed restoration follows the quoted source and explains the human capacity in which illness and recovery occur. The same source confirms the positive بما يقع, already reported from Dubai XVI:649. Ṭībī's exact damaged wording still requires another witness; no new scan collation is claimed.]","source":"C07 review register · T113.07","scope":[{"record_id":"K113.05","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"113:4","segment_title":"al-Falaq · Al-naffāthāt are women, souls, or groups of female sorcerers who tie knots in threads, blow upon the"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-25","title":"T114.07 · C07 correction","content":"The Arabic is حَقَقْتُ الشئَ أَحُقُّه, form I, not ḥaqqaqtu (form II).\n\nBefore: ḥaqqaqtu al-shayʾa aḥuqquhu\n\nAfter: ḥaqaqtu al-shayʾa aḥuqquhu","source":"C07 review register · T114.07","scope":[{"record_id":"K114.05","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"114:6","segment_title":"al-Nās · “From among jinn and humankind” explains “who whispers,” on the basis that Satan is of two kinds, ji"}]},{"id":"c07-correction-26","title":"T114.07 · C07 correction","content":"Primary quoted source establishes intended criticism but not exact Tibi emendation.\n\nBefore: [REVIEW T114-U3: The final sentence is problematic in the print itself: وهذا القول المتعسف لا يريد أنه ضعيف. Its wording is retained, even though its denial of weakness conflicts with the following criticism. The phrase لا يريد may be displaced from the next gloss; that is a hypothesis, not an adopted correction. Check the Imam's source before repairing it. Mā in وما أحقه is negative, not an exclamation; the scan, Ṭībī's gloss and Turkish p.1480 agree.]\n\nAfter: [SOURCE CHECK T114-U3: The earlier scan report gives وهذا القول المتعسف لا يريد أنه ضعيف. Al-Rāzī's tafsīr of 114:6 instead reads وهذا القول ضعيف, followed by the same linguistic objection. This confirms that the source criticizes the interpretation, but does not establish how the corruption arose in Ṭībī. The printed wording is retained with this warning; displacement from the next gloss remains a hypothesis. Mā in وما أحقه is negative, not exclamatory.]","source":"C07 review register · T114.07","scope":[{"record_id":"K114.05","segment_index":2,"quran_context":"114:6","segment_title":"al-Nās · “From among jinn and humankind” explains “who whispers,” on the basis that Satan is of two kinds, ji"}]}];
